<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peugeot</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peugeot is a French car maker founded by an industrial family from the Montbéliard area, Lutherans like most of the region's people. The family turned a mill into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> foundry in 1810, making saws, tools, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> mills and springs, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bicycle">bicycles</a> and, from 1889, motor vehicles. Its main plant is at Sochaux, and the brand now belongs to the Stellantis group.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Peugeot family of Valentigney, near Montbéliard in Franche-Comté, began manufacturing in 1810 with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> foundry, which soon made saws and other hand tools. Around 1840 to 1842 it added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> grinders. In the 1850s it introduced waist corsets made with light steel blades, in 1874 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> grinders, and about 1880 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bicycle">bicycles</a>. On 20 November 1858 Émile Peugeot applied for the lion trademark, and in 1882 Armand Peugeot introduced the penny-farthing called Le Grand Bi, with a range of other bicycles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Armand Peugeot became interested in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/automobile">automobile</a> early, after meeting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottlieb-daimler">Gottlieb Daimler</a> and others. The first Peugeot car, a three-wheeled steam car designed by Léon Serpollet, appeared in 1889, and only four were made, since steam power was heavy and slow to warm up. In 1890, after meeting Daimler and Émile Levassor, Peugeot abandoned steam for a four-wheeled car with a petrol engine built by Panhard under Daimler licence. One was sold to the young Alberto Santos-Dumont, who exported it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. Automobiles Peugeot was created in 1896. The company had a long record in racing, including victories at the Indianapolis 500 in 1913, 1916 and 1919.</p><p>During the Second World War Peugeot made armoured vehicles for the German war effort, while its management was generally sympathetic to the French Resistance. Workers sabotaged production, and Rudolphe and Jean-Pierre Peugeot lent one hundred thousand francs to the Special Operations Executive agent Harry Rée. After a bombing raid in July 1943 that left 110 dead, management agreed with Rée to continue sabotage at the Sochaux plant to avoid further bombing. Car production restarted in 1946 with the 202, and the 203 of 1947 remained in production until 1960. The 403 of 1955 sold a million copies, and the 404 of 1960 won the East African Safari Rally four times.</p><p>In 1974 Peugeot bought 30% of Citroën and took it over completely in 1975, forming the PSA Peugeot Citroën group. The Peugeot 205, launched in 1983, is largely credited with turning the company's fortunes around, and the 206 became the best-selling Peugeot model of all time, with 8,358,217 cars sold by 2012.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Peugeot's Sochaux plant is still a large manufacturing site and houses the Peugeot Museum. Peugeot cars have won six European Car of the Year awards, the first for the 504 in 1969 and the second for the 405 in 1988.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peugeot” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The region kept its Lutheran heritage and later became the home of the <b>Peugeot</b> industrial dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-montbeliard">County of Montbéliard</a></li></ul></section>
